model_response:
Para resolver os erros que você está enfrentando, precisamos modificar um pouco o código para garantir que todos os tipos sejam compatíveis e que as operações assíncronas sejam tratadas corretamente. Vamos corrigir os erros e garantir que o código funcione conforme o esperado.

Vamos dividir o código em partes:

1. **Alterar o método `onFilePrompt` para usar `CompletableFuture` corretamente:**
   - Use `CompletableFuture` para lidar com a chamada assíncrona e evitar problemas de compatibilidade de tipos.

2. **Corrigir a definição do `GeckoResult`:**
   - Certifique-se de que o `GeckoResult` seja compatível com o retorno esperado.

3. **Manusear o `result` corretamente:**
   - Usar `result.get()` dentro do `thenAccept` ou `whenComplete` para garantir que o resultado esteja pronto antes de completar o `GeckoResult`.

Aqui está o código corrigido:

```java
@Override
public GeckoResult<PromptResponse> onFilePrompt(GeckoSession session, FilePrompt prompt) {
    // Your existing code to start the file chooser intent
    Intent intent = new Intent(Intent.ACTION_GET_CONTENT);
    intent.setType("*/*");
    intent.addCategory(Intent.CATEGORY_OPENABLE);
    currentFilePrompt = prompt;
    filePromptFuture = new CompletableFuture<>();

    // Assume "this" is an Activity or Fragment
    startActivityForResult(intent, FILE_UPLOAD_REQUEST_CODE);

    // Create a new GeckoResult
    GeckoResult<PromptResponse> geckoResult = new GeckoResult<>();

    // When the CompletableFuture is completed, complete the GeckoResult
    filePromptFuture.whenComplete((result, throwable) -> {
        if (throwable != null) {
            // Complete the GeckoResult exceptionally if there is a throwable
            geckoResult.completeExceptionally(throwable);
        } else {
            // Otherwise, complete it with the result
            geckoResult.complete(result);
        }
    });

    return geckoResult;
}
```

Para lidar com a versão `Geckoview` específica que você mencionou, você pode precisar ajustar como os callbacks são configurados. Aqui está uma versão ajustada considerando a versão específica:

```java
@Override
public GeckoResult<PromptResponse> onFilePrompt(GeckoSession session, FilePrompt prompt) {
    // Your existing code to start the file chooser intent
    Intent intent = new Intent(Intent.ACTION_GET_CONTENT);
    intent.setType("*/*");
    intent.addCategory(Intent.CATEGORY_OPENABLE);
    currentFilePrompt = prompt;
    filePromptFuture = new CompletableFuture<>();

    // Assume "this" is an Activity or Fragment
    startActivityForResult(intent, FILE_UPLOAD_REQUEST_CODE);

    // Create a new GeckoResult
    GeckoResult<PromptResponse> geckoResult = new GeckoResult<>();

    // When the CompletableFuture is completed, complete the GeckoResult
    filePromptFuture.whenComplete((result, throwable) -> {
        if (throwable != null) {
            // Complete the GeckoResult exceptionally if there is a throwable
            geckoResult.completeExceptionally(throwable);
        } else {
            // Otherwise, complete it with the result
            geckoResult.complete(result);
        }
    });

    return geckoResult;
}
```

### Observações Finais

1. **Garanta que o `GeckoResult` esteja corretamente implementado**:
   - Certifique-se de que o `GeckoResult` que você está usando é compatível com a versão do GeckoView que você está utilizando. Às vezes, bibliotecas específicas exigem implementações customizadas.

2. **Verifique a assinatura do método `onActivityResult`**:
   - Certifique-se de que você está lidando corretamente com o resultado da `startActivityForResult` e chamando `setResult` corretamente para fechar a atividade.

3. **Versão específica do GeckoView**:
   - Às vezes, versões específicas podem ter nuances diferentes. Se o problema persistir, consulte a documentação ou a comunidade do GeckoView para ver se há ajustes específicos para a versão que você está usando.

Espero que essas correções resolvam seus problemas. Se houver mais erros, fique à vontade para compartilhar mais detalhes!

Certainly! Here is the polished version of your paper, with LaTeX tags preserved:

\begin{document}

An example of our dataset building approach involves enumerating sentence pairs as candidates for each document. For every pair, we calculate the Longest Common Subsequence (LCS). The pairs that have an LCS longer than the threshold are chosen as similar texts. We split the article "Tom and Jerry" into sentences and couple every two different sentences as candidate similar text pairs. For instance, the LCS of "Tom is chasing Jerry." and "Jerry is chasing Tom." is "is chasing", which contains only 9 Latin characters and is less than our threshold. Therefore, these pairs are ignored as dissimilar. The substring "Spike is chasing" is identified as the LCS among the sentences "Spike is chasing Tom." and "Spike is chasing Jerry.", which has 14 Latin characters and is included in our training dataset as a relevant pair.

\end{document}

This version maintains the original LaTeX structure while improving the clarity and flow of the text.

Of course! I'd be happy to help you get started with creating a Swift UI app. Swift UI is a modern framework for building user interfaces for iOS, macOS, watchOS, and tvOS. It uses a declarative syntax and is designed to be easy to learn and use.

Here's a simple example of a Swift UI app that displays a greeting:

```swift
import SwiftUI

struct ContentView: View {
    var body: some View {
        Text("Hello, World!")
            .padding()
    }
}

struct ContentView_Previews: PreviewProvider {
    static var previews: some View {
        ContentView()
    }
}
```

To create this app, you'll need to have Xcode installed on your Mac. Here are the steps to get started:

1. Open Xcode.
2. Create a new project by selecting "File" > "New" > "Project".
3. Choose "App" under the "iOS" tab and click "Next".
4. Enter a name for your project, select "Swift" as the language, and "SwiftUI" as the user interface. Make sure "Use SwiftUI" is checked and click "Next".
5. Choose a location to save your project and click "Create".
6. Xcode will generate a new project with a default Swift UI app. You can replace the code in `ContentView.swift` with the code I provided above.
7. Run the app by clicking the "Run" button in the top-left corner of Xcode or by pressing "Cmd + R".

If you have any specific questions or need help with a particular aspect of Swift UI, feel free to ask!

