In LaTeX, you can create a new line within a cell of a table using the `p{}` column type in the `tabular` environment. The `p{}` column type allows you to specify a paragraph (p) column with a fixed width. Inside this type of column, you can use the `\newline` command to create a new line.

Here is an example of how to create a table with a cell that spans multiple lines:

```latex
\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{array}

\begin{document}

\begin{tabular}{|p{3cm}|p{3cm}|p{3cm}|}
\hline
\textbf{Column 1} & \textbf{Column 2} & \textbf{Column 3} \\ \hline
First line & Second line & Third line \\ \hline
This cell has \\ multiple lines & Another cell with \\ multiple lines & And another cell \\ \hline
\end{tabular}

\end{document}
```

In this example:
- The `p{3cm}` column type is used for each column, specifying a width of 3cm.
- Within the cell that needs multiple lines, the `\newline` command is used to create a new line.

If you want more flexibility, you can use the `tabularx` package, which allows you to specify the total width of the table and automatically adjusts the column widths:

```latex
\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{tabularx}

\begin{document}

\begin{tabularx}{\textwidth}{|X|X|X|}
\hline
\textbf{Column 1} & \textbf{Column 2} & \textbf{Column 3} \\ \hline
First line & Second line & Third line \\ \hline
This cell has \\ multiple lines & Another cell with \\ multiple lines & And another cell \\ \hline
\end{tabularx}

\end{document}
```

In this example:
- The `tabularx` environment is used instead of `tabular`.
- The `X` column type is used, which automatically adjusts to the available width.
- Again, `\newline` is used within the cells to create new lines.

These methods ensure that the table is formatted properly and that the content within cells can span multiple lines.

Certainly! To find duplicates in a collection in C#, you can use various methods. Below, I'll show you a simple and efficient way using LINQ (Language Integrated Query) to find duplicates in an array or a list of objects.

Let's assume you have a collection of objects and you want to find duplicates based on a specific property. Here's an example where we have a list of `Person` objects, and we want to find duplicates based on their `Name` property.

First, define your `Person` class:

```csharp
public class Person
{
    public string Name { get; set; }
    public int Age { get; set; }
}
```

Now, you can create a function to find duplicates:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Linq;

public class Program
{
    public static void Main()
    {
        List<Person> people = new List<Person>
        {
            new Person { Name = "John", Age = 30 },
            new Person { Name = "Jane", Age = 25 },
            new Person { Name = "John", Age = 30 },
            new Person { Name = "Doe", Age = 22 },
            new Person { Name = "Jane", Age = 25 }
        };

        var duplicates = FindDuplicates(people, p => p.Name);

        foreach (var duplicate in duplicates)
        {
            Console.WriteLine($"Duplicate found: {duplicate.Name}");
        }
    }

    public static IEnumerable<Person> FindDuplicates<T>(IEnumerable<T> collection, Func<T, object> keySelector)
    {
        var seen = new HashSet<object>();
        var duplicates = new List<T>();

        foreach (var item in collection)
        {
            var key = keySelector(item);
            if (seen.Contains(key))
            {
                if (!duplicates.Any(i => Equals(i, item)))
                {
                    duplicates.Add(item);
                }
            }
            else
            {
                seen.Add(key);
            }
        }

        return duplicates;
    }
}
```

In this example:
1. We define a `Person` class with `Name` and `Age` properties.
2. We create a list of `Person` objects and add some duplicates.
3. We define the `FindDuplicates` method:
   - `T` is the type of the collection elements.
   - `keySelector` is a function that extracts the key to check for duplicates (in this case, the `Name` property).
   - We use a `HashSet` to keep track of the keys we've seen so far.
   - We iterate through the collection and check if the key has been seen before.
   - If it has, we add the item to the `duplicates` list if it's not already there.
   - Finally, we return the list of duplicates.

When you run this code, it will output:
```
Duplicate found: John
Duplicate found: Jane
```

This method is efficient and easy to understand. You can modify the `keySelector` parameter to match the property or criteria you want to use for finding duplicates. If you want to find duplicates based on multiple properties, you can adjust the `keySelector` to return a tuple or a composite key.

Creating a VHDL UART transmitter involves several steps. We need to define the UART interface, handle the data transmission, and manage the clock division. Below is an example of a simple UART transmitter in VHDL, which takes an 8-bit word as input and outputs a signal based on a configurable clock divider. The transmission is started by a high pulse on the W input.

First, let's define the entity and architecture for the UART transmitter.

```vhdl
library IEEE;
use IEEE.STD_LOGIC_1164.ALL;
use IEEE.NUMERIC_STD.ALL;

entity UART_Transmitter is
    generic (
        CLOCK_DIVIDER : integer := 16  -- Configurable clock divider
    );
    port (
        CLK : in  std_logic;          -- Input clock
        W   : in  std_logic;          -- Start of transmission signal (one cycle high)
        TX  : out std_logic         -- UART Transmit line (output)
    );
end UART_Transmitter;

architecture Behavioral of UART_Transmitter is
    type StateType is (IDLE, START_BIT, DATA_BITS, STOP_BIT);
    signal State : StateType := IDLE;
    signal DataReg : std_logic_vector(7 downto 0); -- 8-bit data register
    signal TransmitBuffer : std_logic_vector(8 downto 0); -- 9-bit transmit buffer (including start and stop bits)
    signal TransmitClock : std_logic;
    signal TransmitEnable : std_logic;
    signal BitCounter : integer range 0 to 9 := 0; -- Bit counter for 9 bits (start + 8 data + stop)

begin
    -- Clock divider process
    process(CLK)
    begin
        if rising_edge(CLK) then
            TransmitClock <= not TransmitClock;
        end if;
    end process;

    -- UART transmitter process
    process(TransmitClock, TransmitEnable)
    begin
        if TransmitEnable = '1' then
            case State is
                when IDLE =>
                    TransmitBuffer <= "10000000"; -- Start bit
                    State <= START_BIT;
                    BitCounter <= 1;

                when START_BIT =>
                    TransmitBuffer <= DataReg; -- 8 data bits
                    State <= DATA_BITS;
                    BitCounter <= 2;

                when DATA_BITS =>
                    TransmitBuffer <= TransmitBuffer(7 downto 0) & TransmitBuffer(8);
                    State <= STOP_BIT;
                    BitCounter <= 3;

                when STOP_BIT =>
                    TransmitBuffer <= "10000001"; -- Stop bit
                    State <= IDLE;
                    BitCounter <= 0;
            end case;
        else
            TransmitBuffer <= TransmitBuffer;
            if BitCounter > 0 then
                BitCounter <= BitCounter - 1;
            end if;
            TransmitEnable <= '0';
        end if;
    end process;

    -- Output logic
    process(TransmitClock)
    begin
        if rising_edge(TransmitClock) then
            if BitCounter = 0 then
                TX <= TransmitBuffer(8);
            else
                TX <= TransmitBuffer(8 - BitCounter);
            end if;
        end if;
    end process;

    -- Data register update process
    process(W, CLK)
    begin
        if rising_edge(CLK) then
            if W = '1' then
                DataReg <= '1' & CLK & CLK & CLK & CLK & CLK & CLK & CLK & CLK; -- 8-bit data
                TransmitEnable <= '1';
            end if;
        end if;
    end process;

end Behavioral;
```

### Explanation:

1. **Entity Definition**: The entity `UART_Transmitter` has a generic parameter `CLOCK_DIVIDER` which can be configured to set the clock division ratio. It has three ports: `CLK` for the input clock, `W` to start the transmission, and `TX` for the UART output.

2. **State Machine**: The state machine manages the UART transmission process with states `IDLE`, `START_BIT`, `DATA_BITS`, and `STOP_BIT`.

3. **Clock Divider**: A simple clock divider process generates a `TransmitClock` that is the input clock divided by 2.

4. **Transmit Process**: This process handles the state transitions and the bit transmission. It starts with a start bit, followed by 8 data bits, and ends with a stop bit. The `TransmitEnable` signal is set when the `W` input is high to initiate the transmission.

5. **Output Logic**: This process outputs the bits from the `TransmitBuffer` in the correct order based on the `BitCounter`.

6. **Data Register Update**: This process updates the `DataReg` with the 8-bit data when the `W` input is high.

### Note:
- The `W` input is used to start the transmission. When `W` is high, the data is loaded into the `DataReg` and the `TransmitEnable` is set to start the transmission.
- The `TransmitEnable` is reset to '0' after the transmission is complete.
- The `BitCounter` helps manage the timing of each bit transmission.
- The clock divider ratio is set to 2 in this example. You can change the `CLOCK_DIVIDER` generic parameter to adjust the division ratio.

This example assumes a basic UART with start, 8 data bits, and stop bits. Adjustments might be needed for different baud rates or additional features like parity.

The issue you're encountering is due to the way the format specifier `%s` in `sscanf` works. When you use `%s`, it reads a sequence of characters until it encounters a whitespace character (space, tab, newline, etc.) and stores them in the buffer you've provided (`arg` in your case). If `str` contains `]` after the sequence of characters you're trying to read, `sscanf` will stop reading at the first whitespace it encounters, which is the space after `foo`, and the `]` will remain in the buffer.

To correctly read only "foo" and ignore the rest, you can modify the format string to specify that you only want to read characters up to the `]` character. You can do this by using a custom format specifier that limits the number of characters read. Here's how you can do it:

```c
sscanf(str, "bleh [%99s]", arg);
```

In this example, `99` is the maximum number of characters you want to read. If the string `str` is "bleh [foo]", `arg` will be set to "foo" and the rest of the string will be ignored. If you want to ensure that you read up to the first `]`, you can use a combination of `%99[^\]]` to read up to the first `]` character:

```c
sscanf(str, "bleh [%99[^\]]", arg);
```

This format specifier will read up to the first `]` character, which will include everything up to but not including the `]`. Note that this requires the C library version to support the `%99[^\]]` format specifier. If your C library does not support this, you might need to use a different approach, such as using `strtok` or `strncpy` to extract the substring.

Alternatively, if you want to ensure that you read up to the first `]` and then remove the `]` from the result, you can use:

```c
char temp[100];
sscanf(str, "bleh [%99[^\]]", temp);
strncpy(arg, temp, 99);
arg[99] = '\0';
```

This will read the substring up to the first `]` into `temp` and then copy it to `arg`, ensuring that `arg` is null-terminated.

Choose the method that best fits your needs and the version of C you are using.

